    heroImage: {"src": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/7009459/pexels-photo-7009459.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940", "alt": "A globe placed on a black suitcase symbolizing global travel and adventure.", "caption": "Photo by Tima Miroshnichenko on Pexels"},
    sections: [{"h2": "Two products for different jobs", "paras": ["Travel insurance and expat health insurance often get lumped together because both involve medical cover abroad. They are designed for different situations, though. Travel insurance assumes that you will return home, and that the main risks are emergencies, delays, cancellations and lost belongings.", "Expat insurance assumes that you live abroad, which means you need coverage for ordinary medical needs such as consultations and ongoing prescriptions, not only accidents. The price and structure reflect that."]}, {"h2": "What travel insurance usually focuses on", "paras": ["Typical features include emergency medical treatment, trip cancellation, baggage loss and sometimes evacuation. Many policies set a maximum trip length per journey and may restrict repeated trips or stays in your country of residence.", "Routine or planned care is often excluded. So are pre-existing conditions unless declared and accepted. If you plan to stay for many months, look closely at the fine print."]}, {"h2": "What expat health insurance usually focuses on", "paras": ["Expat plans aim at continuing healthcare. They can include outpatient visits, hospital treatment, prescriptions, maternity options and mental health, depending on the tier. Many include evacuation and repatriation.", "Because they cover more, premiums are usually higher than for travel cover. They can also come with underwriting questions about your health history and with waiting periods for some benefits."]}, {"h2": "Questions to ask before you buy", "paras": ["Use the same checklist for each product."], "list": ["Maximum length of stay and whether the policy renews while abroad", "Whether routine and chronic conditions are covered", "How pre-existing conditions are treated", "Annual and per-condition limits", "Whether the policy meets visa or residence requirements", "Where you are covered, including your home country and the United States", "How claims work: direct billing or reimbursement"]}, {"h2": "Matching cover to your situation", "paras": ["A person on a two-week trip is well served by travel insurance. A person who relocates for several years will usually need long-term cover, possibly through a local scheme once residency is established.", "In between sit slow travellers, remote workers and people who move between countries. For them, nomad-oriented plans or flexible expat plans may fit, as long as the wording matches their lifestyle. Be honest in applications, since inaccurate answers can lead to claims being denied."]}, {"h2": "Using local public healthcare", "paras": ["Some countries allow residents to join public health systems, sometimes by paying a contribution. That can be a cost-effective route, but it often applies only after you have a particular residency status. Until then, private insurance may be needed.", "Check with the relevant government health authority and keep documents that prove your enrolment, since visa renewals may ask for them.", "Renewal is another point worth checking. Some expat policies renew annually with premiums that rise with age or claims history, while some travel policies cannot be extended once you have left home. If your plans might change, ask how renewal works, whether you can extend cover from abroad, and what happens if your health changes during the policy year."]}, {"h2": "Read the documents, not the ads", "paras": ["Marketing pages highlight benefits, while the policy wording lists limits and exclusions. Download the full terms before you pay, and ask the insurer to explain anything unclear in writing.", "This article is general information, not insurance or medical advice. Confirm details with licensed providers and official government sources."]}],
