    title: "Tax Residency Basics: What the 183-Day Rule Is and Why It Is Not the Whole Story",
    excerpt: "A general explanation of tax residency for expats and digital nomads: the 183-day idea, other residency tests, tie-breaker rules and why you should confirm with an adviser.",
    category: "taxes",
    tags: ["tax residency", "183 day rule", "expat taxes", "digital nomad taxes", "double taxation"],
    date: "2026-11-01",
    readTime: 4,
    coverColor: "#3B0764",
    author: "Expat Checkbook Editorial",
    wordCount: 720,
    quickAnswer: "Tax residency decides which country can tax your worldwide income. Many countries use a day-count test, often around 183 days, but most also look at other factors such as home, family ties, economic interests and intent. Rules differ by country, so check official tax authority guidance or speak with a qualified adviser.",
    faq: [{"q": "Does 183 days always make me a tax resident?", "a": "No. It is common in many systems, but countries define residency in their own ways and may use other tests as well."}, {"q": "Can I be a tax resident in two countries?", "a": "Yes, it can happen. Tax treaties often include tie-breaker rules to decide which country has priority."}, {"q": "Does leaving a country automatically end tax residency?", "a": "Not necessarily. Some countries require you to show that you have cut ties, and some use specific departure rules."}, {"q": "Does tax residency affect my visa?", "a": "They are separate systems. A visa grants permission to stay, while tax residency concerns taxation. Both may apply at once."}, {"q": "Who can tell me my residency status?", "a": "The national tax authority and qualified tax advisers. Online articles cannot give personal advice."}],
    heroImage: {"src": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/6927334/pexels-photo-6927334.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940", "alt": "Mobile calculator app on tax documents, highlighting financial calculations and tax preparation.", "caption": "Photo by Polina Tankilevitch on Pexels"},
    sections: [{"h2": "Residency is a legal label, not a feeling", "paras": ["Tax residency is the status a country gives you for tax purposes. Once you are a resident, that country may tax your income from anywhere in the world, while non-residents are usually taxed only on income sourced in that country.", "That is why people who move abroad, work remotely or travel often should understand it. Without that knowledge, it is possible to end up with tax obligations in more than one place, or to assume that you owe nothing when you do."]}, {"h2": "The 183-day idea", "paras": ["Many countries use a day-count test, and 183 days is a common figure, roughly half a year. If you spend more than that in a country during a tax year, you may be considered a resident there.", "However, the details differ. Some countries count days in a calendar year, others in a fiscal year that begins in April or July. Some count part-days, and some use a rolling period across several years. Treat 183 as a rule of thumb, not a guarantee."]}, {"h2": "Other factors that can matter", "paras": ["Many tax systems look beyond the number of days. Authorities may consider a combination of facts."], "list": ["Where your permanent home is", "Where your spouse and children live", "Where your main work or business is based", "Where your personal and economic interests are centred", "Your citizenship, which matters in a few systems", "Your declared intention to stay"]}, {"h2": "Dual residency and tie-breakers", "paras": ["It is possible for two countries to treat you as a resident at the same time. Double taxation treaties often include tie-breaker rules to decide which country has the primary right to tax. They commonly look at permanent home, centre of vital interests, habitual abode and nationality in a defined order.", "Applying these rules requires facts and sometimes formal certificates. Not every pair of countries has a treaty, and treaty terms differ. This is a topic where professional advice usually pays for itself."]}, {"h2": "Citizenship-based taxation", "paras": ["A small number of countries tax citizens on worldwide income regardless of where they live. The best-known example is the United States. Citizens of such countries may still have to file returns while abroad, though credits and exclusions may reduce actual tax.", "If you hold citizenship in more than one country, check the rules in each. Do not assume that moving away ends your obligations."]}, {"h2": "Keeping records", "paras": ["Keep a clear record of where you spent your days, using passport stamps, flight confirmations, accommodation bookings and calendar notes. Records also support visa applications and residence permits.", "Save documents that show where your home, family and work are. If a tax authority asks questions, evidence helps. Consider a simple spreadsheet that tracks days by country, updated monthly.", "Employers add another layer. If you work remotely for a company abroad, your presence in a country may affect the employer's obligations, such as payroll withholding or even corporate tax presence. Some companies have policies limiting how long staff can work from another country for this reason. Check with your employer or clients before settling somewhere for an extended period."]}, {"h2": "Next steps", "paras": ["Read the official guidance from the tax authority of each country involved, and speak to a qualified adviser who understands cross-border situations. Ask for written advice if you are making big decisions, such as moving or changing employers.", "This article is general information, not tax or legal advice. Rules change, so confirm the details with official sources."]}],
